        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Final Formatting"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "FINAL_FORMATTING",
          "id": "1763542278096",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1763542278097_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Take this rest response as input {{$context.$nodes.READ_TASK_FILE.$output}} \nconst readTaskFileResponse = {{$context.$nodes.READ_TASK_FILE.$output}} \n\nTake this rest response as input {{$context.$nodes.READ_RULES_FILE.$output}}\nconst readRulesFileResponse = {{$context.$nodes.READ_RULES_FILE.$output}}\n\nYou are given two API responses: one from readTaskFile and one from readRulesFile.\nYour job is to generate a structured summary describing each participant and the rules they use.\n\nStep 1 — Identify Participants (from readTaskFile)\nLook in readTaskFileResponse.result.nodeArray.\nEvery node with a ruleSetName represents a participant.\nExtract:\nid → participant name\nparticipantType → SERIALPARALLEL\nruleSetName → rule set name\nignoreParticipant → “yes” or “no”\nSkip participants where ignoreParticipant = \"yes\".\n\nStep 2 — Find Rules (from readRulesFile)\nLook in readRulesFileResponse.result.finalJsonResult\nMatch the participant’s ruleSetName with the corresponding key inside\nInside that rule set, pick only nodes with \"type\": \"RULE\".\n\nStep 3 — Extract Details for Each Rule\nFor each rule node:\nRule name (ruleName)\nStatus (ruleNodeStatus or active)\nCondition: Convert the rule’s logical expression (ruleString) into a short, human-readable statement describing when this rule triggers. Avoid parentheses, AND/OR operators, or code-like syntax. Use phrases like “when…”, “if…”, “applies when…”. For multiple conditions, combine them into a readable sentence.\nAction type or outcome (if available)\nAssociated approvers or routing logic (from next \"PARTICIPANT\" nodes)\nlistBuilderType (e.g. ResListBuilder, SupListBuilder)\nresListBuilderRepresentativeType, resListBuilderRepresentativeOf, etc and Rewrite into a short, human-readable statement\nBut these are not new participants — they describe how approval is routed inside the rule.\n\nStep 4 — Write the Output\n\nWrite one paragraph per main participant only (from task file).\nInside each participant’s paragraph, describe their rule set and rules as bullet points.\nApply bold formatting where <B> tag is used\n\nOutput Format Example\n\nParticipant SoaOLabel.GoalApprovalRoutingParticipants is SERIAL and not ignored.\nIt uses the rule set PerformanceGoalApprovalRule, which defines the following rules:\n\n• PerformanceGoalAutoApproval (Inactive)\n<B>Condition</B>: Applies when the goal is pending approval and marked as private, and the transaction attribute25 is set to \"Y\n<B>Approver routing</B>: Approvers are determined via the management hierarchy (Route using LINE_MANAGER), starting with the requestor’s manager and up to the approver specified in Task.payload.transactionApprovalRequest.attribute16 (number of levels = 1); action is Approval Required\n\n• PerformanceGoalManualApproval (Inactive)\n<B>Condition</B>: Applies when the goal is pending approval, not private, and the transaction attribute25 is \"Y\".\n<B>Approver routing</B>: Approvers are HR_REP representatives drawn from Task.payload.getRepresentativeListResponse.result, and the action is Approval Required\n\nOutput Rules\nDo not repeat participant info for every rule.\nTreat “ResListBuilder”, “SupListBuilder”, or “Automatic Approval” as approval routing methods, not participants.\nKeep one clean paragraph per participant, with bullet points for rules.\nAvoid markdown (**, #, *) and emojis.\nWrite plain, structured text only."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        }
      ],
